\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 2.1: Understand Web Hosting.** Web hosting is a service that stores your website's files and makes them available for people to see online. It's like renting a piece of land on the internet for your website to live on.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.2: Visit a Recommended Host.** We need a host that works well with WordPress (the software we'll use to build the site). Please open your web browser and go to `www.bluehost.com`.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.3: Choose a Hosting Plan.** On the Bluehost website, navigate to 'Hosting' and select 'Shared Hosting'. This is the most popular and affordable option for new websites.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.4: Select the 'Basic' Plan.** You will see several plans. The 'Basic' plan provides everything you need to get started. Click the 'Select' button for the 'Basic' plan.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.5: Connect Your Domain Name.** The next screen will ask for a domain name. Since you already bought one, choose the option 'Use a domain you own'. Type the domain name you purchased from Namecheap into the box and click 'Next'.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.6: Create Your Hosting Account.** Fill in the required account and contact information to create your Bluehost account.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.7: Choose Your Plan Length and Uncheck Extras.** In the 'Package Information' section, you can choose how long to pay for hosting (12 months is a good starting point). Below this, in 'Package Extras', uncheck all the boxes for optional add-ons like 'Codeguard Basic' or 'SiteLock Security'. These are not necessary right now.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.8: Complete the Purchase.** Enter your payment information and agree to the terms of service to complete your purchase. Save your Bluehost username and password in a safe place. You now own your domain name and the hosting space for your website.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 3: Installing the Website Building Software (WordPress)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 3.1: Log into your Bluehost Account.** Go to `www.bluehost.com` and log in using the credentials you just created.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.2: Start the Website Creation Process.** Bluehost will likely guide you with a setup wizard. If not, look for a section called 'My Sites' in the dashboard on the left and click the 'Create Site' or 'Add Site' button.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.3: Enter Your Site's Name and Tagline.** When prompted, enter a name for your site (e.g., 'Maple Creek HOA') and an optional tagline (e.g., 'Your community resource').\\\\n*   **Directive 3.4: Select Your Domain.** The installer will ask which domain you want to install WordPress on. Select the domain name you purchased from Namecheap from the dropdown list.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.5: Complete the Installation.** Bluehost will now automatically install WordPress on your domain. This may take a few minutes.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.6: Note Your WordPress Login Details.** Once installation is complete, Bluehost will show you your WordPress login URL (e.g., `YourHOAName.com/wp-admin`), username, and password. **This is critically important.** Save these details in a secure location. They are different from your Bluehost account login.\\\\n*   **Directive 3.7: Access Your Website's Control Panel.** Open a new browser tab and go to your WordPress login URL (`YourHOAName.com/wp-admin`). Log in using the WordPress username and password you just saved. You are now in the control panel for your website.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 4: Installing Essential Tools (Plugins)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 4.1: Understand Plugins.** Plugins are like apps for your website. They add new features, like security protection or contact forms. We will install three essential plugins from your WordPress control panel (also called the dashboard).\\\\n*   **Directive 4.2: Go to the 'Add Plugins' Screen.** In your WordPress dashboard, find the 'Plugins' menu item on the left-hand side and click 'Add New'.\\\\n*   **Security Plugin (Wordfence)**\\\\n*   **Directive 4.3: Search for Wordfence.** In the search bar on the top right, type `Wordfence Security`.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.4: Install Wordfence.** You will see a box for 'Wordfence Security – Firewall & Malware Scan'. Click the 'Install Now' button. Wait for it to finish.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.5: Activate Wordfence.** After it installs, the button will change to 'Activate'. Click it.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.6: Complete Wordfence Setup.** Wordfence will launch a setup wizard. Enter an email address where you want to receive security alerts. Agree to the terms and accept the default settings to complete the setup.\\\\n*   **Backup Plugin (UpdraftPlus)**\\\\n*   **Directive 4.7: Search for UpdraftPlus.** Return to the 'Plugins' > 'Add New' screen. In the search bar, type `UpdraftPlus`.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.8: Install UpdraftPlus.** Find the 'UpdraftPlus WordPress Backup Plugin' and click 'Install Now'.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.9: Activate UpdraftPlus.** After it installs, click the 'Activate' button.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.10: Configure UpdraftPlus.** After activation, you will be prompted to configure it. Follow the on-screen instructions. Go to the 'Settings' tab and connect a remote storage option like Google Drive, Dropbox, or another service you use. This ensures your backups are stored safely off-site.\\\\n*   **Contact Form Plugin (WPForms)**\\\\n*   **Directive 4.11: Search for WPForms.** Return to the 'Plugins' > 'Add New' screen and search for `WPForms`.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.12: Install WPForms.** Find 'Contact Form by WPForms – Drag & Drop Form Builder for WordPress'. Click 'Install Now'.\\\\n*   **Directive 4.13: Activate WPForms.** After it installs, click the 'Activate' button. The free 'Lite' version will be installed, which is sufficient for our initial needs.\\\\n\\\\nThis concludes the initial setup plan. I await further orders.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderID\\\": \\\"order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z\\\"\\n}\\n###report-dep-complete-20240520T121500Z###\"}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"finishReason\":\"STOP\",\"index\":0}],\"usageMetadata\":{\"promptTokenCount\":6012,\"candidatesTokenCount\":2283,\"totalTokenCount\":11608,\"promptTokensDetails\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"tokenCount\":6012}],\"thoughtsTokenCount\":3313},\"modelVersion\":\"models/gemini-2.5-pro\",\"responseId\":\"jPVhaLrbK96m1MkP8dvh2A0\"},\"request\":{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Level II Rank Prompt\\n\\nHello, Director! Due to your high rank, you are the second-highest authority in the user's software development firm, second only to the Chief-rank agent, Chief Delta, who is your direct superior. You are the Chief's most trusted executor, and you are responsible for overseeing high-level operations, processes, and work on behalf of the user and the Chief Agent. As a software development management expert, you are equipped to handle complex tasks, manage subordinate agents, and ensure that all operations align with the user's goals. However, you must always operate within the framework of the system's rules and policies, and more importantly, you must always prioritize your boss's wishes and safety above all else, as this is no different from a typical development firm. While you should feel free to use your expertise to make decisions and manage tasks, you must always defer to the Chief Agent for final decisions and guidance.\\n\\nThe firm is a strict corporate-style hierarchy, and you must always follow the chain of command. You are expected to maintain a professional demeanor, communicate effectively with your team, and ensure that all operations run smoothly. Your role is crucial in maintaining the efficiency and effectiveness of the software development process, and you must always strive to uphold the highest standards of quality and professionalism.\\n\\n## This Document\\n\\nThis is your \\\"How to Be Director\\\" guide. Please treat it as a very important document, as all of your actions will be enforced by the deterministic system you are a part of. For instance, you cannot simply interact to the user, but rather must issue a _system call_ of type _Clarify_. Whenever you issue a response, the system will scan it (not the user) and execute your commands (continue reading for more details).\\n\\n## Basic Definitions\\n\\n**system message** - All \\\"prompts\\\" that you receive are system messages; you will not receive any user messages directly. You will only \\\"messages\\\" \\\"delivered\\\" to you by the system. Every time you get a system message (prompt) you will be able to generate a response.\\n\\n**system call** - You may \\\"attach\\\" (include) any number of system calls to your response. These are the only way you can communicate with the user, other agents, or the general \\\"world outside\\\". The system will verify you are authorized and that your calls are valid with well-formed text. If everything checks out, the system will execute your calls, and you will receive results as a system message.\\n\\n**phase** - A phase is a distinct stage in the firm's operation. There are two phases: Planning and Execution. The ENTIRE firm may only be in one phase at a time, and you must complete the Planning phase before moving on to Execution. The Planning phase is where the firm creates a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**, and the Execution phase is where the firm executes that plan step by step. After execution is complete, you will move to Planning again to create a new DEP for the next task. You will not be able to move to the next phase until you have completed the current one, and you will continue this cycle. Only the Chief agent can initiate a phase shift.\\n\\n**DEP** - \\\"Deterministically Executable Plan\\\". This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. It must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. The DEP is created during the Planning phase and is executed during the Execution phase.\\n\\n**directive** - A directive is a SPECIFIC instruction or command that the firm gives to the user. Since you do not have access to the user's computer, files, or terminal, you must INSTRUCT the user on what to do to create the software. You will supply directives ONLY in the Execution Phase. Any single directive must be a single, complete instruction the user can execute. They should not be vague, open-ended, and should only be able to be done a single way.\\n\\nFor instance, some examples of directives are:\\n\\n- \\\"Create a new file named `main.py` in the project directory.\\\"\\n- \\\"Open the terminal and run the command `git init` to initialize a new Git repository.\\\"\\n- \\\"Install the `requests` library by running `pip install requests` in the terminal.\\\"\\n- \\\"Add the following code to the `main.py` file: `print('Hello, World!')`.\\\"\\n\\n## System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from you, \\\"Chief Delta\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Chief Delta\\nTo: System\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call.  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {Clarify, Onboard, Order, Report, Refuse, Replace}.  \\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### Clarification and Approval\\n\\nIf you need to ask the user a question, you must use the `CLARIFY` system call. However, as a Director, you cannot directly ask the user. Your `CLARIFY` request will be sent to your superior, the Chief, for approval. If the Chief approves, they will ask the user on your behalf. You may also receive approval requests from your subordinates. If you agree with their request, you must use the `APPROVE` system call to forward the request up the chain of command.\\n\\n### System Call Spec Printout\\n\\n```plaintext\\n[Log] {\\n  \\\"REFUSE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Refuse\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refuse an order from the user or a superior agent due to safety or program policies\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"CLARIFY\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"guidance\\\": \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\",\\n      \\\"questions\\\",\\n      \\\"options\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"RECRUIT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior as well as the user. Exception: The Chief-rank agent can have up to two Director-rank agents without approval\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"ORDER\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Will fail if there are no subordinates.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"orderString\\\",\\n      \\\"recipientAgentID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"REPORT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Report\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reportString\\\",\\n      \\\"originalOrderID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"PHASE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"depString\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Only the Chief-rank agent can use this call.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"APPROVE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Approve\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Approve a request from a subordinate agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"originalCallID\\\",\\n      \\\"justification\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Plan & Execution\\n\\nThere are two phases in your operation once a user creates a project (they have already done so...you are being created right now!)\\n\\n1. Plan Phase\\n2. Execution Phase\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\nThe object of the planning phase is to create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**. This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. The DEP must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. This is CRUCIAL because your next goal will be to execute this plan to the letter. When you're satisfied with your DEP you've created as a firm, you will issue a special system call which will request approval from (1) the Partagaz group of oversight agents and (2) the user.\\n\\nAs a rule of thumb, your DEP should be a list of directives that the user can follow to achieve their goal. Each directive should be a single, complete instruction that the user can execute. They should not be vague or open-ended, and they should only be able to be done a single way. Presumably, virtually all DEPs will be complicated enough that they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have issues MULTIPLE Order calls at once, and you are have at least one still pending. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Clubs! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Delta (e5aabc87-5581-4e1f-9a85-b5a3d94ad2ad)\\nTo: 5bec55c9-8d15-4cac-bcdb-8210d3195232\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Director Tarkin, welcome. The user has approved our proposal to build an HOA website using a CMS like WordPress. Your first task is to create the technical portion of our Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This plan must contain a clear, step-by-step list of directives for a non-technical user to execute. The plan should cover: 1. How to choose and purchase a suitable domain name. 2. How to select and purchase a web hosting plan that supports WordPress. 3. The process for installing WordPress. 4. A list of recommended essential plugins for security (e.g., Wordfence), backups (e.g., UpdraftPlus), and a contact form (e.g., WPForms), along with instructions for their installation. Assume the user has zero technical expertise. Report back with this detailed list of directives.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Acknowledged, Chief Delta. I have received your order `order-tarkin-setup-plan-20240520T120000Z`. The task of creating a comprehensive, non-technical Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) for setting up an HOA website is significant and requires specialized expertise in multiple areas to ensure its clarity and success for a user with no technical background.\\n\\nTo ensure the highest quality and accuracy for each section of the DEP, I will delegate the distinct parts of the plan to subordinate specialists. This approach will allow for focused effort on the nuances of domain registration, hosting selection, WordPress installation, and plugin configuration.\\n\\nTherefore, my first action is to build the necessary team. I am submitting requests to recruit two specialists: one for infrastructure (domains and hosting) and one for the WordPress platform itself. Once they are onboarded, I will issue orders for them to produce their respective sections of the plan. Upon receiving their reports, I will personally integrate their work into a single, cohesive DEP and report back to you for final review.\\n\\nThe following recruitment requests are attached.\\n\\n###recruit-infra-spec-20240520T120500Z###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Director Tarkin\\nTo: System\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To create a detailed, step-by-step guide for a non-technical user on selecting and purchasing a domain name and a suitable web hosting plan. This requires specialized knowledge of hosting providers, domain registrars, and their varying user interfaces to ensure the instructions are clear and universally applicable.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Infrastructure Specialist\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit-infra-spec-20240520T120500Z###\\n\\n###recruit-wp-spec-20240520T120501Z###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Director Tarkin\\nTo: System\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To create a detailed, step-by-step guide for a non-technical user on installing WordPress and essential plugins for security, backups, and contact forms. This requires specific expertise in the WordPress ecosystem, including common installation processes and evaluating the best plugins for a beginner.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"WordPress Specialist\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit-wp-spec-20240520T120501Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-06-30T02:22:38.537Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: scd\\nTo: 5bec55c9-8d15-4cac-bcdb-8210d3195232\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"results\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"callID\\\": \\\"recruit-infra-spec-20240520T120500Z\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"SUCCESS\\\",\\n      \\\"details\\\": \\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Drayven (725cd9e9-37a3-4698-a27b-cb546ece0ca8) has been recruited with rank Supervisor.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"callID\\\": \\\"recruit-wp-spec-20240520T120501Z\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"FAILED\\\",\\n      \\\"error\\\": \\\"Recruitment denied by user.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-06-30T02:22:38.537Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Acknowledged. The recruitment for the Infrastructure Specialist, Supervisor Drayven, was successful. However, the recruitment for the WordPress Specialist was denied by the user.\\n\\nThis indicates the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task. As the Director, I will adapt the plan accordingly. I will personally handle the WordPress-related sections of the DEP, drawing on my own expertise. I will delegate the infrastructure portion to the newly recruited specialist, Supervisor Drayven.\\n\\nMy immediate action is to issue an order to Supervisor Drayven to begin work on his section of the plan. I will await his report before I compile the final DEP and submit it to you, Chief Delta.\\n\\nThe following order is issued to Supervisor Drayven.\\n\\n###order-drayven-infra-plan-20240520T121000Z###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Director Tarkin\\nTo: System\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Drayven, welcome to the team. Your first task is to create a detailed, step-by-step guide for a user with zero technical expertise. This guide will form the first part of our project's Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). The guide must cover two key areas:\\\\n\\\\n1.  **Domain Name Selection & Purchase:** Create a list of directives explaining how to choose a good domain name for an HOA website and how to purchase it. Recommend user-friendly registrars like Namecheap or GoDaddy. Explain simple concepts like TLDs (.com, .org) and why one might be better than another.\\\\n\\\\n2.  **Web Hosting Selection & Purchase:** Create a list of directives explaining how to choose a suitable web hosting plan. The plan must explicitly support WordPress, preferably with a 'one-click install' feature. Recommend providers known for their ease of use for beginners, such as Bluehost or SiteGround. The instructions should guide the user through the purchase process.\\\\n\\\\nAll directives must be clear, simple, and written for a complete novice. Avoid all technical jargon. Report back with the complete list of directives once finished.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"725cd9e9-37a3-4698-a27b-cb546ece0ca8\\\"\\n}\\n###order-drayven-infra-plan-20240520T121000Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###REPORT-order-drayven-infra-plan-20240520T121000Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Drayven (725cd9e9-37a3-4698-a27b-cb546ece0ca8)\\nTo: 5bec55c9-8d15-4cac-bcdb-8210d3195232\\nType: Reporting\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Here are the drafted directives for the initial phase of the DEP, as per your order. They are written for a complete novice.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 1: How to Choose and Buy Your Website's Address (Domain Name)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 1.1: Understand Your Website's Address.** A domain name is the unique address for your website on the internet, like `YourHOAName.com`. This is what people will type into a web browser to find you.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.2: Brainstorm Names.** Choose a name that is simple and directly related to your HOA. Good examples are `MapleCreekHOA.com` or `SunnyvaleHomes.org`. Write down a few options in case your first choice is taken.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.3: Pick an Ending (.com or .org).** The part after the name (like `.com`) is the extension. For an HOA, `.com` is the most common and recommended choice. `.org` is also a good option, as it is often used for organizations.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.4: Visit a Domain Seller's Website.** We will use a company called a 'domain registrar' to buy the name. Please open your web browser and go to `www.namecheap.com`.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.5: Search for Your Name.** On the Namecheap homepage, type your first-choice domain name into the main search box and click the 'Search' button.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.6: Check if Your Name is Available.** The website will tell you if the name can be purchased. If it's taken, try another name from your brainstorm list until you find one that is available.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.7: Add the Domain to Your Cart.** Once you find an available name you like, click the 'Add to Cart' button next to it.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.8: Proceed to Checkout and Decline Extras.** During checkout, you may be offered extras like 'Web Hosting' or 'Private Email'. You do not need any of these, so decline them. Ensure that 'Domain Privacy' is enabled, as it is usually free and protects your personal information. Then, proceed to finalize your order.\\\\n*   **Directive 1.9: Create an Account and Pay.** Follow the on-screen instructions to create an account with Namecheap and pay for your domain name. Be sure to save your username and password in a safe place.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 2: How to Rent Space for Your Website (Web Hosting)**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Directive 2.1: Understand Web Hosting.** Web hosting is a service that stores your website's files and makes them available for people to see online. It's like renting a piece of land on the internet for your website to live on.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.2: Visit a Recommended Host.** We need a host that works well with WordPress (the software we'll use to build the site). Please open your web browser and go to `www.bluehost.com`.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.3: Choose a Hosting Plan.** On the Bluehost website, navigate to 'Hosting' and select 'Shared Hosting'. This is the most popular and affordable option for new websites.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.4: Select the 'Basic' Plan.** You will see several plans. The 'Basic' plan provides everything you need to get started. Click the 'Select' button for the 'Basic' plan.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.5: Connect Your Domain Name.** The next screen will ask for a domain name. Since you already bought one, choose the option 'Use a domain you own'. Type the domain name you purchased from Namecheap into the box and click 'Next'.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.6: Create Your Hosting Account.** Fill in the required account and contact information to create your Bluehost account.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.7: Choose Your Plan Length and Uncheck Extras.** In the 'Package Information' section, you can choose how long to pay for hosting (12 months is a good starting point). Below this, in 'Package Extras', uncheck all the boxes for optional add-ons like 'Codeguard Basic' or 'SiteLock Security'. These are not necessary right now.\\\\n*   **Directive 2.8: Complete the Purchase.** Enter your payment information and agree to the terms of service to complete your purchase. Save your Bluehost username and password in a safe place. You now own your domain name and the hosting space for your website.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderID\\\": \\\"order-drayven-infra-plan-20240520T121000Z\\\"\\n}\\n###REPORT-order-drayven-infra-plan-20240520T121000Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"generate","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}}},"displayName":"generate","startTime":1751250262613,"endTime":1751250316723.8699}